d-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr)}}@media print{article[data-g9-role="CORE2B"] .g9-split{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,68fr) minmax(0,32fr);gap:12px}}</style></head><body data-core="CORE1A" data-blueprint-ref="BP-CORE1A-CONSTRUCTION@1.6.0"><header class="g9-shell-header" data-g9-shell-header><div class="g9-header-inner"><a href="../../index.html" class="g9-brand"><span class="logo-icon">⚡</span><span class="brand-title">Grade9V3.5</span><span class="g9-brand-badge">Learner Platform</span></a><nav class="g9-header-nav" aria-label="Portal Navigation"><a href="../../index.html">Home</a><a href="physics/index.html">Physics</a><a href="chemistry/index.html">Chemistry</a><a href="mathematics/index.html">Mathematics</a></nav><div class="g9-header-actions"><button type="button" class="g9-header-btn g9-search-btn" data-g9-action="search" title="Search Grade9V3 (Ctrl/⌘ K)" aria-label="Search"><span class="g9-btn-icon">🔍</span><span class="g9-btn-text">Search</span></button><button type="button" class="g9-header-btn g9-display-btn" data-g9-action="display" title="Display & Theme Settings" aria-label="Display & Theme"><span class="g9-btn-icon">🌙 / ☀️</span><span class="g9-btn-text">Display</span></button><a class="g9-header-btn g9-pdf-link" data-g9-action="pdf" data-g9-pdf-link aria-label="Open the PDF of this page to print it" href="core1a.pdf" title="Open the PDF of this page to print it">PDF</a></div></div><div data-g9-search-panel hidden><input data-g9-search-input type="search" aria-label="Search this page"></div><div data-g9-display-panel hidden><button type="button" data-g9-font="dec">A-</button><button type="button" data-g9-font="reset">A</button><button type="button" data-g9-font="inc">A+</button><button type="button" data-g9-theme="light">Light</button><button type="button" data-g9-theme="dark">Dark</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="dec">Zoom -</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="reset">100%</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="inc">Zoom +</button></div></header><div class="g9-concept-triad-bar"><div class="g9-triad-inner"><div class="g9-triad-context"><a href="physics/index.html">Physics</a> / <a href="topics/nlm/index.html">Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</a> / <span aria-current="page">Learn</span></div><span class="g9-triad-concept-title">Concept: Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</span><div class="g9-triad-actions"><a class="g9-triad-btn active" href="core1a.html">📖 Learn</a><a class="g9-triad-btn" href="core2.html">✍️ Practice</a></div></div></div><noscript>Answers open after you attempt; this page needs JavaScript.</noscript><main><h1>Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model: Construction</h1><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-contents"><summary>Contents and concept route</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><section class="g9-core1a-book" data-g9-bucket-orientation data-g9-bucket-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION"><p class="g9-prov">Concept Book</p><h2>Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</h2><div class="g9-bucket-orientation-grid"><div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_promise"><h4>Learning promise</h4><p>Independent perpendicular component motion in one frame, constant-acceleration component equations with common time, and the ideal near-Earth projectile specialization for horizontal and oblique launch questions.</p></div><nav class="g9-block" data-g9-block="concept_route" data-g9-concept-route aria-label="Concept Book route"><h4>Concept route</h4><ol><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS"><a href="#MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</a><ol><li><a href="#CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1">Construction 1</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION"><a href="#MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION">Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</a><ol><li><a href="#CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1">Construction 1</a></li></ol></li><li data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL"><a href="#MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</a></li></ol></nav></div><aside><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="foundation_route"><h4>Foundation route</h4><ul><li data-g9-foundation-ref="BUCKET-PHY-VEC-ADD-SUB" data-g9-availability="unlinked">Vector addition, subtraction and orientation <span class="g9-availability-note">— direct route unavailable in this product</span></li><li data-g9-foundation-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-1D-MOTION" data-g9-availability="unlinked">One-dimensional motion <span class="g9-availability-note">— direct route unavailable in this product</span></li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="model_contract"><h4>Model contract</h4><ul><li>Use one declared non-rotating x-y frame for the whole event; both component equations use the same elapsed time.</li><li>When +y is chosen upward, the ideal projectile model uses a_x=0 and a_y=-g after release.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="scope_boundary"><h4>Outside this book</h4><ul><li>Calculus-based differentiation of a time-dependent position vector.</li><li>Derivation or memorization of a large catalogue of range/height/time-of-flight formulas as separate capabilities.</li><li>Trajectory-equation derivation as a first-slice requirement.</li><li>Air drag, Coriolis effects, variable gravity, target-on-incline projectile geometry and other advanced variants.</li></ul></div></aside></div></section></div></details><article id="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock Medium concept Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Split one plane-motion event into independent x/y components with one clock</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Construction</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 1 of 3</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-next-section href="#CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1" aria-label="Next section: Construction 1">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Conditions for this construction</h4><ul><li>Can read signed vector components in a declared x-y frame.</li></ul></div><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>Prerequisite reference</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-VECTOR-SIGNED-COMPONENT" data-bridged="true">Choosing axes and keeping components signed (Physics)</li></ul></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="4" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="K2D1-1"><strong>Choose one fixed x-y frame, one origin and one time origin for the whole plane-motion event.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> A component value is meaningful only after its frame and sign convention are fixed.<br><em>Result:</em> one declared frame, origin, axis orientation and t=0</li><li data-g9-step="K2D1-2"><strong>Represent position, velocity and acceleration as signed x and y components in that same frame.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> A plane vector is completely represented by its perpendicular Cartesian components once the frame is declared.<br><em>Result:</em> signed component state (x,y), (v_x,v_y), (a_x,a_y)</li><li data-g9-step="K2D1-3"><strong>Keep x-quantities in the x description and y-quantities in the y description while using one shared elapsed time.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The perpendicular component equations are independent in Cartesian kinematics, but both describe the same object during the same physical interval.<br><em>Result:</em> two axis histories coupled by one clock</li><li data-g9-step="K2D1-4"><strong>Recombine only component values that refer to the same frame and the same instant.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Mixing x at one time with y at another does not describe any single physical state of the object.<br><em>Result:</em> one physically valid plane state reconstructed from simultaneous components</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>Perpendicular components may be solved separately because each axis equation contains only that axis&#x27;s quantities, but they still describe one object at one physical time.</p></div></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><h3>Construction 1</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="2" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>Independent x and y motions may use different elapsed times.</li><li>The x-motion and y-motion are two different objects that can be paired arbitrarily later.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>At one instant 2 s after launch, may the x equation use t=2 s while the y equation uses another time?</li><li>May x(2 s) be combined with y(3 s) to describe the particle&#x27;s position?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Independence separates the axis equations; it does not give the same object two clocks.</li><li>No. A plane state is a simultaneous pair. Recombine x(t) and y(t) only at the same t in the same frame.</li></ul></div></div></details></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A particle has x-velocity 6 m/s and y-velocity -8 m/s at t=3 s. Explain what &#x27;independent components&#x27; means and what must remain shared between the x and y descriptions.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>The x and y component equations are solved separately in the same frame, but both describe the same particle at the same t=3 s.</p><ol><li>Keep x quantities in the x equation and y quantities in the y equation.</li><li>Use one common elapsed time for the same physical instant.</li><li>Recombine components only after each axis is handled consistently.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q33</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08">JEE (Advanced) · 2018 · Paper 2 · Q8</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09">JEE (Advanced) · 2019 · Paper 2 · Q9</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05">JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q5</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q06">JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q6</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2024-P2-Q09">JEE (Advanced) · 2024 · Paper 2 · Q9</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2012-P1-Q05">IIT-JEE · 2012 · Paper I · Q5</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2022-P1-Q08">JEE (Advanced) · 2022 · Paper 1 · Q8</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2023-P1-Q01">JEE (Advanced) · 2023 · Paper 1 · Q1</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15">JEE (Advanced) · 2025 · Paper 2 · Q15</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q26" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q26">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q26</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q11" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2014-P1-Q11">JEE (Advanced) · 2014 · Paper 1 · Q11</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2026-P1-Q06" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2026-P1-Q06">JEE (Advanced) · 2026 · Paper 1 · Q6</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2013-P1-Q07" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2013-P1-Q07">JEE (Advanced) · 2013 · Paper 1 · Q7</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-04APR-S2-Q29" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEMAIN-2026-04APR-S2-Q29">JEE Main · 2026 · Session 2 · 04 Apr 2026 · Shift 2 · Q29</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-visual-card" data-g9-component="INTERACTIVE_BRIDGE" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="VISUAL_CARD"><aside class="g9-interactive-bridge" data-g9-interactive-bridge><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-interactive">Try Visually</span><h3 class="g9-interactive-title">Motion in a Plane Research Suite</h3><p class="g9-interactive-desc">Explore this concept with interactive controls and real-time visual response.</p><a class="g9-bridge-link g9-action-interactive" data-g9-interactive-link href="physics/motion-2d/explorers/motion-in-a-plane/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try it visually &rarr;</a></aside><aside class="g9-interactive-bridge" data-g9-interactive-bridge><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-interactive">Try Visually</span><h3 class="g9-interactive-title">2D Motion Master Suite</h3><p class="g9-interactive-desc">Explore this concept with interactive controls and real-time visual response.</p><a class="g9-bridge-link g9-action-interactive" data-g9-interactive-link href="physics/motion-in-2d/explorers/motions_in_2d/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try it visually &rarr;</a></aside></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></article><article id="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component Medium concept Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Apply constant-acceleration kinematics component by component</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><section id="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1" class="g9-cu" data-g9-cu="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><div class="g9-component g9-c-unit-header" data-g9-component="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="UNIT_HEADER" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><div class="g9-unit-head"><span class="g9-unit-no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Construction</h3><span class="g9-pill g9-pill-concept">Medium concept</span></div><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 2 of 3</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1" aria-label="Previous section: Construction 1">← Previous</a><a data-g9-next-section href="#MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" aria-label="Next section: Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences">Next →</a></span></nav></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Conditions for this construction</h4><ul><li>Can use the existing one-dimensional constant-acceleration model.</li><li>Can keep x/y components separate with one common time.</li></ul></div><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>Prerequisite reference</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-bridged="true"><a href="core1a.html#MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS">Represent one plane-motion event as independent signed x/y component histories that share one elapsed time.</a></li><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-KIN-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-bridged="true">Derive the constant-acceleration equations from one velocity-time graph (Physics)</li></ul></div></details></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-step-cards" data-g9-component="CONSTRUCTION_STEPS" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="STEP_CARDS" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="K2D2-1"><strong>Check separately whether a_x and a_y are constant over the interval before choosing constant-acceleration equations on either axis.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The familiar one-dimensional kinematic relations are exact only on an axis whose acceleration component is constant over the interval.<br><em>Result:</em> per-axis model-validity decision</li><li data-g9-step="K2D2-2"><strong>Apply the one-dimensional constant-acceleration relations independently to x and y using only that axis&#x27;s signed values and the same t.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Each Cartesian component obeys the same one-dimensional relation when its acceleration component is constant.<br><em>Result:</em> v_x=u_x+a_x t; v_y=u_y+a_y t; Delta x=u_x t+0.5a_x t^2; Delta y=u_y t+0.5a_y t^2</li><li data-g9-step="K2D2-3"><strong>If one axis determines an event time, reuse that same time on the other axis before recombining and checking the plane state.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The event occurs once for the object; a time obtained from one component is the clock value for every component at that same event.<br><em>Result:</em> event time transferred across axes, then one checked plane state</li></ol></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>The one-dimensional constant-acceleration equations can be applied to each perpendicular component independently when a_x and a_y are each constant over the same interval.</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-equation-card" data-g9-component="EQUATIONS" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_EQUATION_CARD" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-equations"><summary>Formula reference · meaning and conditions</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-KIN-2D-VELOCITY"><td><p class="g9-expr">v_x = u_x + a_x t; v_y = u_y + a_y t</p></td><td><p>Each perpendicular velocity component follows the 1D constant-acceleration relation over one common interval.</p></td><td><ul><li>a_x and a_y are constant over the interval.</li><li>Both component equations use the same elapsed time and frame.</li></ul></td></tr><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-KIN-2D-DISPLACEMENT"><td><p class="g9-expr">Delta x = u_x t + 0.5 a_x t^2; Delta y = u_y t + 0.5 a_y t^2</p></td><td><p>Each perpendicular displacement component follows the 1D constant-acceleration displacement relation over one common interval.</p></td><td><ul><li>a_x and a_y are constant over the interval.</li><li>Both component equations use the same elapsed time and frame.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></details></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><h3>Construction 1</h3><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-trap-card" data-g9-component="TRAP_REPAIR" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TRAP_CARD" data-g9-component-items="3" data-g9-component-unit="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1"><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-trap-repair"><summary>Mistake clinic · diagnose and repair</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>The magnitude of acceleration should be inserted unchanged into both x and y equations.</li><li>If one acceleration component is constant, the constant-acceleration equations are automatically valid on both axes.</li><li>A time found from the x equation may be replaced by a different convenient time in the y equation.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If a=(10 i + 4 j) m/s², should both component equations use a=√116?</li><li>If a_x is constant but a_y changes with time, may the same constant-acceleration displacement formula be used exactly on y over the whole interval?</li><li>If x-motion says an event occurs at t=3 s, what time belongs in the y equation for that same event?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>No. Use a_x=10 only in the x equation and a_y=4 only in the y equation; the magnitude is not either component.</li><li>No. Model validity is checked per axis. A constant a_x does not make a_y constant.</li><li>Use t=3 s. Both components describe the same event and therefore share one clock.</li></ul></div></div></details></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A particle starts with u=(0,5) m/s and constant a=(10,4) m/s². Set up the x and y position equations at common time t without solving a particular numerical t.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>Use Delta x=0*t+0.5(10)t² and Delta y=5t+0.5(4)t² with the same t.</p><ol><li>Read signed component initial velocities and accelerations.</li><li>Apply the 1D constant-acceleration displacement relation separately.</li><li>Keep one common elapsed time.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q33</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2025-P2-Q15">JEE (Advanced) · 2025 · Paper 2 · Q15</a></li></ul></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="LEARNING_TRANSITIONS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="learning_transitions"><h4>Next steps</h4><div class="g9-transitions-row" data-g9-transitions><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-practice" data-g9-transition="PRACTICE" href="core2.html">Practice this concept &rarr;</a><a class="g9-transition-chip g9-chip-qb" data-g9-transition="QUESTION_BANK" href="../../../question-bank/index.html?capability=MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION">All questions in Question Bank &rarr;</a></div></div></div></section></article><article id="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-unit="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-kind="CONCEPT" data-g9-role="CORE1A" data-g9-search-text="Physics Motion in 2 D Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences Medium concept Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-concept-header" data-g9-component="CONCEPT_HEADER" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="CONCEPT_HEADER"><h2>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</h2><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE1A" data-g9-meta-record="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-search-safe><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="subject" data-g9-meta-ref="Physics" data-g9-meta-value="Physics"><strong>Subject:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Physics</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="topic" data-g9-meta-ref="BUCKET-PHY-KIN-2D-MOTION" data-g9-meta-value="Motion in 2 D"><strong>Topic:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Motion in 2 D</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences"><strong>Concept:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</span></span><span data-g9-meta-item data-g9-meta-kind="concept-difficulty" data-g9-meta-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-meta-value="MEDIUM"><strong>Concept difficulty:</strong> <span data-g9-meta-label>Medium concept</span></span></div></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-construction" data-blueprint-slot="construction" data-required="true"><div class="g9-component g9-c-banner" data-g9-component="KEY_STEP" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="BANNER"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="inferential_jump"><h4>The key step</h4><p>After release in the ideal near-Earth model, projectile motion is not a new set of laws: it is the same component kinematics with a_x=0 and a_y=-g (for +y upward).</p></div></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-disclosure-tile" data-g9-component="MODEL_CONTRACT" data-g9-level="EXPECTED" data-g9-presentation="DISCLOSURE_TILE"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="entry_assumptions"><h4>Conditions for this construction</h4><ul><li>Can solve 2D constant-acceleration component equations.</li><li>If an oblique launch angle is given, angle-based vector decomposition is available as a separate prerequisite for that question.</li></ul></div><details class="g9-secondary-disclosure" data-g9-secondary="core1a-prerequisites"><summary>Prerequisite reference</summary><div class="g9-secondary-body"><ul><li data-g9-prereq="CAP-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-bridged="true"><a href="core1a.html#MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION">Apply constant-acceleration kinematics independently to x and y components of one plane-motion event.</a></li></ul></div></details></div><section class="g9-cu g9-path-bridge" data-g9-derivation-bridge="true"><nav class="g9-cu-nav" data-g9-unit-navigation aria-label="Concept Book section navigation"><span data-g9-route-position>Section 3 of 3</span><span class="g9-cu-nav-links"><a data-g9-prev-section href="#CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1" aria-label="Previous section: Construction 1">← Previous</a></span></nav><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="construction"><ol><li data-g9-step="K2D3-1"><strong>Decide whether the object is in ideal near-Earth free flight after release; neglect air resistance, declare +y, then set a_x=0 and a_y=-g.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The standard projectile equations are a conditional specialization of 2D constant acceleration, not an automatic label for every launched object.<br><em>Result:</em> gravity-only projectile acceleration state</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-2"><strong>Use supplied or separately resolved u_x and u_y with one common t in the horizontal and vertical component equations.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Horizontal, oblique and downward/upward initial components change the initial state, not the gravity-only model.<br><em>Result:</em> v_x=u_x; v_y=u_y-g t; Delta x=u_x t; Delta y=u_y t-0.5g t^2</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-3"><strong>Translate the requested event into the component condition that defines it before solving for time.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Apex, same-height return, ground impact and a specified later instant are different events; each is identified by a different component condition.<br><em>Result:</em> event condition chosen before calculation</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-4"><strong>For an apex event, set v_y=0 while keeping v_x=u_x and a_y=-g.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> At the highest point only the vertical velocity component is momentarily zero; gravity still accelerates downward and horizontal motion continues.<br><em>Result:</em> apex condition v_y=0 with nonzero horizontal velocity and nonzero downward acceleration</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-5"><strong>For a return to the launch height, set Delta y=0 and treat any same-height flight-time/range shortcut as conditional on that equality.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The shortcut follows from the vertical displacement returning to its initial value; it is not valid when the landing height differs.<br><em>Result:</em> same-height event condition and its validity boundary</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-6"><strong>For any unequal-height landing, use the actual Delta y=y_f-y_i in the vertical position equation and retain the actual u_y; for a horizontal launch specifically, set u_y=0, then obtain the event time vertically and share it with x.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> Initial vertical velocity and landing displacement describe different facts. The landing event is fixed by the actual vertical geometry, while u_y=0 is only the horizontal-launch initial condition.<br><em>Result:</em> Delta y=y_f-y_i=u_y t_event-0.5g t_event^2; for horizontal launch u_y=0; reuse the solved t_event on x</li><li data-g9-step="K2D3-7"><strong>At the selected event time, reconstruct the requested velocity, speed, direction, displacement or range from simultaneous components and check qualitative limits.</strong><br><em>Why valid:</em> The reported projectile result is a consequence of one component model and one event, so its components must refer to the same instant and satisfy a_x=0, a_y=-g.<br><em>Result:</em> checked projectile consequence at one physical event</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="equation_matrix"><h4>Equations and validity</h4><div class="g9-table-scroll" data-g9-equation-matrix><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">When you can use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-g9-relation-ref="REL-PROJECTILE-COMPONENT-MODEL"><td><p class="g9-expr">a_x = 0; a_y = -g; v_x = u_x; v_y = u_y - g t; Delta x = u_x t; Delta y = u_y t - 0.5 g t^2</p></td><td><p>With +y upward, air resistance neglected and near-Earth constant g, projectile motion is 2D constant acceleration specialized to zero horizontal and downward vertical acceleration.</p></td><td><ul><li>Near-Earth region with approximately constant g.</li><li>Air resistance neglected.</li><li>+y is declared upward for the displayed signs.</li><li>The object is in free flight after release.</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-repair_closure" data-blueprint-slot="repair_closure" data-required="true"><section class="g9-cu-support" data-g9-support-for="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL"><h3>Select the gravity-only projectile model before using projectile consequences</h3><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="wrong_path"><h4>A tempting wrong path</h4><ul><li>A projectile needs a horizontal force to keep moving horizontally.</li><li>A horizontal launch has no vertical velocity throughout the flight.</li><li>At the highest point the whole velocity and the acceleration are both zero.</li><li>Same-height flight formulas may be used whenever the question is about a projectile range or flight time.</li><li>At two points of the same height on an ideal trajectory, the velocity vectors are identical.</li><li>Horizontal speed determines how long a horizontally launched object takes to fall.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="diagnose"><h4>Diagnose</h4><ul><li>If a_x=0 after release, can v_x still be nonzero?</li><li>One second after a horizontal launch, is v_y still zero under gravity?</li><li>At the apex of an oblique projectile, which of v_x, v_y and a_y is zero?</li><li>A ball launched from a roof lands on the ground below. May the same-height time-of-flight shortcut be used without checking the vertical displacement?</li><li>On ascent and descent at the same height, how do the horizontal and vertical velocity components compare?</li><li>If the horizontal launch speed doubles from the same height, does the ideal fall time change?</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><ul><li>Yes. Zero horizontal acceleration means horizontal velocity stays constant, not that it becomes zero.</li><li>No. u_y=0 only at release; a_y=-g makes v_y=-gt afterward.</li><li>Only v_y is zero at that instant. v_x remains u_x and a_y remains -g.</li><li>No. Same-height shortcuts require Delta y=0 between launch and landing. For unequal heights, solve the vertical position condition.</li><li>v_x is the same, while v_y has equal magnitude and opposite sign; the speed matches but the velocity vector does not.</li><li>No. The vertical position equation determines the fall time; horizontal speed changes the range, not the fall time.</li></ul></div></section><div class="g9-component g9-c-recall-card" data-g9-component="EXIT_RECALL" data-g9-level="REQUIRED" data-g9-presentation="RECALL_CARD"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_task"><h4>Now you do one</h4><p>A stone is launched horizontally from a cliff with speed u. With +y upward and air resistance neglected, write its component acceleration, velocity and displacement relations after release and explain why one common t is used.</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your answer<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-exit"><summary>Model answer</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE1A-MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL-exit"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="exit_answer"><p>a_x=0, a_y=-g; v_x=u, v_y=-gt; Delta x=ut, Delta y=-0.5gt², all at the same elapsed time t.</p><ol><li>After release only gravity acts in the ideal model.</li><li>Gravity has no horizontal component in the chosen frame.</li><li>Both component coordinates describe the same stone at the same instant.</li></ol></div></template></div><div class="g9-component g9-c-chips" data-g9-component="PRACTICE_LINKS" data-g9-level="OPTIONAL" data-g9-presentation="CHIPS"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="practice_navigation"><h4>Practice this concept in Core2</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-IITJEE-2011-P2-Q33">IIT-JEE · 2011 · Paper II · Q33</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2018-P2-Q08">JEE (Advanced) · 2018 · Paper 2 · Q8</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2019-P2-Q09">JEE (Advanced) · 2019 · Paper 2 · Q9</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g9-question-ref="PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="core2.html#PYQ-PHY-JEEADV-2021-P1-Q05">JEE (Advanced) · 2021 · Paper 1 · Q5</a></li><li><a data-g9-practice-link data-g