ax(0,1fr)}}@media print{article[data-g9-role="CORE2B"] .g9-split{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,68fr) minmax(0,32fr);gap:12px}}</style></head><body data-core="CORE2B" data-blueprint-ref="BP-CORE2B-TRANSFER@1.1.0"><header class="g9-shell-header" data-g9-shell-header><div class="g9-header-inner"><a href="../../index.html" class="g9-brand"><span class="logo-icon">⚡</span><span class="brand-title">Grade9V3.5</span><span class="g9-brand-badge">Learner Platform</span></a><nav class="g9-header-nav" aria-label="Portal Navigation"><a href="../../index.html">Home</a><a href="physics/index.html">Physics</a><a href="chemistry/index.html">Chemistry</a><a href="mathematics/index.html">Mathematics</a></nav><div class="g9-header-actions"><button type="button" class="g9-header-btn g9-search-btn" data-g9-action="search" title="Search Grade9V3 (Ctrl/⌘ K)" aria-label="Search"><span class="g9-btn-icon">🔍</span><span class="g9-btn-text">Search</span></button><button type="button" class="g9-header-btn g9-display-btn" data-g9-action="display" title="Display & Theme Settings" aria-label="Display & Theme"><span class="g9-btn-icon">🌙 / ☀️</span><span class="g9-btn-text">Display</span></button><a class="g9-header-btn g9-pdf-link" data-g9-action="pdf" data-g9-pdf-link aria-label="Open the PDF of this page to print it" href="core2b.pdf" title="Open the PDF of this page to print it">PDF</a></div></div><div data-g9-search-panel hidden><input data-g9-search-input type="search" aria-label="Search this page"></div><div data-g9-display-panel hidden><button type="button" data-g9-font="dec">A-</button><button type="button" data-g9-font="reset">A</button><button type="button" data-g9-font="inc">A+</button><button type="button" data-g9-theme="light">Light</button><button type="button" data-g9-theme="dark">Dark</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="dec">Zoom -</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="reset">100%</button><button type="button" data-g9-zoom="inc">Zoom +</button></div></header><div class="g9-concept-triad-bar"><div class="g9-triad-inner"><div class="g9-triad-context"><a href="physics/index.html">Physics</a> / <a href="topics/nlm/index.html">Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</a> / <span aria-current="page">CORE2B</span></div><span class="g9-triad-concept-title">Concept: Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</span><div class="g9-triad-actions"><a class="g9-triad-btn" href="core1a.html">📖 Learn</a><a class="g9-triad-btn" href="core2.html">✍️ Practice</a></div></div></div><noscript>Answers open after you attempt; this page needs JavaScript.</noscript><main><h1>Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model: Transfer</h1><article id="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-REPRESENTATION-01" data-g9-unit="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-REPRESENTATION-01" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2B" data-g9-search-text="A particle is described verbally: at t=0 it is at the origin, moving east at 6 m/s and north at 2 m/s; during the next interval its acceleration is 3 m/s^2 south and zero east-west. Without being given component equations, choose a coordinate convention and write the signed component state that should be used for later calculation." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="provenance"><p class="g9-prov">AUTHORED transfer</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2B" data-g9-meta-record="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-REPRESENTATION-01" data-g9-meta-incomplete="true"></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><h2>A particle is described verbally: at t=0 it is at the origin, moving east at 6 m/s and north at 2 m/s; during the next interval its acceleration is 3 m/s^2 south and zero east-west. Without being given component equations, choose a coordinate convention and write the signed component state that should be used for later calculation.</h2></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="lineage"><h4>Builds on</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-lineage href="core2a.html#Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2A-SHARED-CLOCK-01">A tracker reports x(t)=4t metres and y(t)=10-2t^2 metres for one particle in one fixed…</a></li></ul></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Use one fixed Cartesian frame.</li><li>The verbal directions must be translated into signed components before calculation.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="safe_support"><h4>Where to start</h4><p>Before writing numbers, which two perpendicular positive directions will you declare?</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your commitment: the model or representation you choose, and your first relation<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-post_attempt" data-blueprint-slot="post_attempt" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2B-Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-REPRESENTATION-01-review"><summary>Review and solution</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2B-Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-REPRESENTATION-01-review"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="changed_demand"><h4>What changed</h4><p>The learner must convert a verbal direction description into signed x/y state without being handed the component representation.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="protected_move"><h4>The deciding move</h4><p>Choose and declare one perpendicular positive-axis convention before converting the directional facts into signed components.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><h4>Answer</h4><p>One valid choice is +x east and +y north, giving u_x=+6 m/s, u_y=+2 m/s, a_x=0 and a_y=-3 m/s^2, all with one common t.</p><ol><li>Declare +x east and +y north.</li><li>Translate each direction into a sign in that frame.</li><li>Keep the same frame and clock for both component histories.</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rubric"><h4>Rubric</h4><ul><li>Declares a single Cartesian frame before assigning signs.</li><li>Translates east/north/south statements into consistent signed components.</li><li>Keeps one common time for the two component histories.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>Any consistently declared perpendicular axis convention is acceptable if all signs are transformed consistently.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><p><a data-g9-repair-ref="K2D1-2" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS" data-g9-repair-target="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1" href="core1a.html#CU-PHY-KIN-2D-INDEPENDENT-COMPONENTS-1">Revisit: Represent position, velocity and acceleration as signed x and y components in that same frame.</a></p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-MODEL-VALIDITY-02" data-g9-unit="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-MODEL-VALIDITY-02" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2B" data-g9-search-text="Over 0&lt;=t&lt;=4 s, a particle has a_x=2 m/s^2 but a_y=3t m/s^2. Decide whether the ordinary constant-acceleration equations may be used exactly on x, on y, on both, or on neither over the whole interval. Explain the model choice before doing any further calculation." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="provenance"><p class="g9-prov">AUTHORED transfer</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2B" data-g9-meta-record="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-MODEL-VALIDITY-02" data-g9-meta-incomplete="true"></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><h2>Over 0&lt;=t&lt;=4 s, a particle has a_x=2 m/s^2 but a_y=3t m/s^2. Decide whether the ordinary constant-acceleration equations may be used exactly on x, on y, on both, or on neither over the whole interval. Explain the model choice before doing any further calculation.</h2></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="lineage"><h4>Builds on</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-lineage href="core2a.html#Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2A-CONSTANT-ACCEL-02">A particle starts with velocity (3 i + 4 j) m/s and has constant acceleration (2 i - 1…</a></li></ul></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>The acceleration components are given as functions of time.</li><li>No numerical integration is required; the task is model selection.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="safe_support"><h4>Where to start</h4><p>Is &#x27;constant acceleration&#x27; one global label here, or must each component be checked over the interval?</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your commitment: the model or representation you choose, and your first relation<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-post_attempt" data-blueprint-slot="post_attempt" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2B-Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-MODEL-VALIDITY-02-review"><summary>Review and solution</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2B-Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-MODEL-VALIDITY-02-review"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="changed_demand"><h4>What changed</h4><p>The learner must decide component-by-component whether the constant-acceleration model is valid instead of being told that it applies.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="protected_move"><h4>The deciding move</h4><p>Decide separately for each axis whether the constant-acceleration condition is satisfied over the whole interval.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><h4>Answer</h4><p>The constant-acceleration equations are exact on x because a_x is constant, but not on y because a_y changes with time over the interval.</p><ol><li>Check each acceleration component separately.</li><li>a_x=2 is constant, so the 1D constant-acceleration x relations are valid.</li><li>a_y=3t varies with time, so the constant-a y relations are not exact over the whole interval.</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rubric"><h4>Rubric</h4><ul><li>Checks x and y acceleration components separately.</li><li>Accepts constant-acceleration equations only on x.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>A constant total direction or a constant x component does not make the y component constant.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><p><a data-g9-repair-ref="K2D2-1" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION" data-g9-repair-target="CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1" href="core1a.html#CU-PHY-KIN-2D-CONSTANT-ACCELERATION-1">Revisit: Check separately whether a_x and a_y are constant over the interval before choosing constant-acceleration equations on either axis.</a></p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-UNEQUAL-HEIGHT-03" data-g9-unit="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-UNEQUAL-HEIGHT-03" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2B" data-g9-search-text="An ideal projectile begins 15 m above level ground with u_x=10 m/s and u_y=10 m/s. Take +y upward and g=10 m/s^2. Find the ground-impact time and horizontal range. Do not assume launch and landing are at the same height." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="provenance"><p class="g9-prov">AUTHORED transfer</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2B" data-g9-meta-record="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-UNEQUAL-HEIGHT-03" data-g9-meta-incomplete="true"></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><h2>An ideal projectile begins 15 m above level ground with u_x=10 m/s and u_y=10 m/s. Take +y upward and g=10 m/s^2. Find the ground-impact time and horizontal range. Do not assume launch and landing are at the same height.</h2></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="lineage"><h4>Builds on</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-lineage href="core2a.html#Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2A-SAME-HEIGHT-05">An ideal projectile is launched and later lands at the same height with u_x=12 m/s,…</a></li></ul></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Air resistance is neglected.</li><li>Ground is 15 m below the launch point.</li><li>The initial velocity components are already resolved.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="safe_support"><h4>Where to start</h4><p>What vertical displacement identifies ground impact relative to the launch point?</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your commitment: the model or representation you choose, and your first relation<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-post_attempt" data-blueprint-slot="post_attempt" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2B-Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-UNEQUAL-HEIGHT-03-review"><summary>Review and solution</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2B-Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-UNEQUAL-HEIGHT-03-review"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="changed_demand"><h4>What changed</h4><p>The learner must replace the familiar same-height condition with an unequal-height event condition and carry the resulting time across axes.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="protected_move"><h4>The deciding move</h4><p>Choose Delta y=-15 m as the impact condition instead of reusing the familiar same-height condition Delta y=0.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><h4>Answer</h4><p>Ground impact occurs at t=3 s and the horizontal range is 30 m.</p><ol><li>Use the vertical impact condition Delta y=-15=10t-5t^2.</li><li>Solve 5t^2-10t-15=0, giving the physical root t=3 s.</li><li>Reuse t=3 s horizontally: Delta x=10(3)=30 m.</li><li>The same-height shortcut would use the wrong event condition here.</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rubric"><h4>Rubric</h4><ul><li>Uses the actual vertical displacement -15 m rather than a same-height shortcut.</li><li>Reuses the physical impact time in the horizontal equation.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>Substitution into the vertical displacement gives -15 m; the nonzero same-height time 2u_y/g=2 s is not the ground-impact time.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><p><a data-g9-repair-ref="K2D3-6" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-repair-target="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="core1a.html#MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Revisit: For any unequal-height landing, use the actual Delta y=y_f-y_i in the vertical position equation and retain the actual u_y; for a horizontal launch specifically, set u_y=0, then obtain the event time vertically and share it with x.</a></p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-PROJECTILE-VALIDITY-04" data-g9-unit="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-PROJECTILE-VALIDITY-04" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2B" data-g9-search-text="An object is launched and, after release, a small rocket motor continues to provide a horizontal thrust that gives a_x=2 m/s^2 while gravity gives a_y=-g. Decide whether the standard ideal projectile specialization a_x=0, a_y=-g is valid and state the correct bounded model to use." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="provenance"><p class="g9-prov">AUTHORED transfer</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2B" data-g9-meta-record="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-PROJECTILE-VALIDITY-04" data-g9-meta-incomplete="true"></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><h2>An object is launched and, after release, a small rocket motor continues to provide a horizontal thrust that gives a_x=2 m/s^2 while gravity gives a_y=-g. Decide whether the standard ideal projectile specialization a_x=0, a_y=-g is valid and state the correct bounded model to use.</h2></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="lineage"><h4>Builds on</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-lineage href="core2a.html#Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2A-HORIZONTAL-LAUNCH-04">A stone is launched horizontally at 15 m/s from a cliff 20 m above level ground. Take…</a></li></ul></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Air resistance is neglected.</li><li>The horizontal rocket thrust remains active after release.</li><li>The question asks for model selection, not a full trajectory calculation.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="safe_support"><h4>Where to start</h4><p>Which post-release assumption makes a_x=0 in the standard projectile model, and is it true here?</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your commitment: the model or representation you choose, and your first relation<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-post_attempt" data-blueprint-slot="post_attempt" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2B-Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-PROJECTILE-VALIDITY-04-review"><summary>Review and solution</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2B-Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-PROJECTILE-VALIDITY-04-review"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="changed_demand"><h4>What changed</h4><p>The learner must reject the familiar projectile specialization because one of its defining force/model conditions is explicitly broken.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="protected_move"><h4>The deciding move</h4><p>Reject the standard gravity-only projectile specialization because its defining horizontal-acceleration condition is violated.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><h4>Answer</h4><p>The standard ideal projectile specialization is not valid because a_x is not zero. Use the more general 2D constant-acceleration model with a_x=2 m/s^2 and a_y=-g for as long as those components remain constant.</p><ol><li>The standard projectile specialization requires gravity-only free flight.</li><li>The active rocket motor adds a retained horizontal acceleration.</li><li>Therefore use the parent 2D constant-acceleration capability rather than the gravity-only projectile specialization.</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rubric"><h4>Rubric</h4><ul><li>Rejects the gravity-only projectile specialization.</li><li>Falls back to the general component constant-acceleration model.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>If the motor is switched off after release and drag remains negligible, the standard a_x=0 projectile model becomes valid from that later instant.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><p><a data-g9-repair-ref="K2D3-1" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-repair-target="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="core1a.html#MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Revisit: Decide whether the object is in ideal near-Earth free flight after release; neglect air resistance, declare +y, then set a_x=0 and a_y=-g.</a></p></div></template></section></div></div></article><article id="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-SAME-HEIGHT-VELOCITY-05" data-g9-unit="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-SAME-HEIGHT-VELOCITY-05" data-g9-kind="QUESTION" data-g9-role="CORE2B" data-g9-search-text="An ideal projectile passes the same height once on the way up and once on the way down. Without calculating the times, compare v_x, v_y, speed and acceleration at those two events." class="g9-stage-support"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-identity" data-blueprint-slot="identity" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="provenance"><p class="g9-prov">AUTHORED transfer</p></div><div data-g9-meta-strip data-g9-meta-role="CORE2B" data-g9-meta-record="Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-SAME-HEIGHT-VELOCITY-05" data-g9-meta-incomplete="true"></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="stem"><h2>An ideal projectile passes the same height once on the way up and once on the way down. Without calculating the times, compare v_x, v_y, speed and acceleration at those two events.</h2></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="lineage"><h4>Builds on</h4><ul><li><a data-g9-lineage href="core2a.html#Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2A-PROJECTILE-APEX-03">An ideal projectile is launched with already-resolved components u_x=12 m/s and u_y=20…</a></li></ul></div></section><div class="g9-split g9-split-primary-only"><div class="g9-col g9-col-primary"><section class="blueprint-slot slot-attempt" data-blueprint-slot="attempt" data-required="true"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="conditions"><h4>Conditions</h4><ul><li>Air resistance is neglected.</li><li>Both events occur on the same trajectory at the same vertical position.</li><li>Take +y upward.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="safe_support"><h4>Where to start</h4><p>At the same height, which horizontal quantity is unchanged throughout ideal flight, and what must differ between ascent and descent?</p></div><div class="g9-attempt" data-g9-attempt-box data-g9-response-type="free_response"><label>Your commitment: the model or representation you choose, and your first relation<textarea data-g9-attempt rows="4"></textarea></label><label class="g9-paper"><input data-g9-paper type="checkbox">I worked this on paper</label><button type="button" data-g9-commit>I have attempted this</button></div></section><section class="blueprint-slot slot-post_attempt" data-blueprint-slot="post_attempt" data-required="true"><details data-g9-reveal data-requires-attempt data-g9-payload-ref="CORE2B-Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-SAME-HEIGHT-VELOCITY-05-review"><summary>Review and solution</summary><div data-g9-payload-slot></div></details><template data-g9-payload="CORE2B-Q-PHY-KIN-2D-2B-SAME-HEIGHT-VELOCITY-05-review"><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="changed_demand"><h4>What changed</h4><p>The learner must compare two equal-height events by component invariants rather than solve a routine time/range calculation.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="protected_move"><h4>The deciding move</h4><p>Choose direct component invariants at equal height as the comparison method instead of solving for the two event times.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="answer"><h4>Answer</h4><p>v_x is the same at both events; v_y has equal magnitude and opposite sign; therefore the speed is the same but the velocity vectors differ. Acceleration is the same (0,-g) at both events.</p><ol><li>Horizontal velocity remains constant because a_x=0.</li><li>At equal height in the same gravity-only trajectory, the vertical speed magnitude matches on ascent and descent, with opposite signs.</li><li>Combining the components gives equal speed magnitudes but different velocity directions.</li><li>Gravity gives the same downward acceleration at both events.</li></ol></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="rubric"><h4>Rubric</h4><ul><li>Keeps v_x unchanged across the two events.</li><li>Gives v_y equal magnitude and opposite sign.</li><li>Distinguishes equal speed from equal velocity.</li></ul></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="independent_check"><h4>Independent check</h4><p>The two velocity vectors cannot be identical because one has upward v_y and the other downward v_y.</p></div><div class="g9-block" data-g9-block="repair"><h4>Repair</h4><p><a data-g9-repair-ref="K2D3-7" data-g9-concept-ref="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" data-g9-repair-target="MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL" href="core1a.html#MIC-PHY-KIN-PROJECTILE-MODEL">Revisit: At the selected event time, reconstruct the requested velocity, speed, direction, displacement or range from simultaneous components and check qualitative limits.</a></p></div></template></section></div></div></article></main><footer data-g9-footer>Physics · Two-dimensional kinematics and projectile model</footer><script>\n(()=>{const q=(s,r=document)=>[...r.querySelectorAll(s)];\nconst store={get:k=>{try{return localStorage.getItem(\'g9-\'+k)}catch(e){return null}},set:(k,v)=>{try{localStorage.setItem(\'g9-\'+k,v);return true}catch(e){return false}},remove:k=>{try{localStorage.removeItem(\'g9-\'+k);return true}catch(e){return false}}};\nconst root=document.documentEle